WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.030
- [Narrator] Hello readers,

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:05.050
today we're talking
about how the delivery of

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.930
the speech affects the
impact of the words.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.750
So what do I mean by that?

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.140
It's all the ways that

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:16.600
how a person says something
affects what they mean.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.510
Words on a page may have
a certain definition,

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:23.540
but we humans use all
sorts of different ways

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.140
to show what we really mean.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.930
We can say something quietly

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:33.200
or really loudly to emphasize a point.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.200
We can emphasize some
words, but not others.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.960
We can pause, to create dramatic tension.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.460
We know that how something
is said is important

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.960
because when we don't
have that information,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.280
sometimes it's hard to know

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.610
what another person is really saying.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:57.000
Say that you forgot about your
friend Eli's birthday party.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
So you text them, "hey,
sorry I missed your party."

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.500
Seriously for me this is one

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.580
of the most anxiety-inducing situations

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.950
that I encounter on a regular basis.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.500
What does Eli actually mean here?

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:16.760
It's fine.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.340
Or it's fine

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.810
or it's fine.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.480
Is it fine or isn't it?

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.660
Gang, I don't know.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.810
Eli's tone and body language,

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.150
which we can't see in just a text message,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.500
add in important elements of meaning.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.770
We can apply that
knowledge to how we speak

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.870
and how we assess the speech of others.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:47.250
Good public speakers use
a range of techniques

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.210
to convey their message to their audience,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.970
which goes beyond just
the words that they say.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:57.970
They become actors who are
telling us a story on stage,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.610
not just with their lines,

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.090
but with their physical presence.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.360
So, let's talk about
some of these techniques

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.210
and then we'll watch a
little bit of a speech

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.480
so you can spot some examples.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.670
First, there's vocal variety.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.760
This means using different speaking speeds

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.120
and changing between soft
and loud speaking volumes.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.190
When people don't do this,

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:25.456
we say that they're speaking
in a monotone, you know.

00:02:25.456 --> 00:02:27.726
Bueller?

00:02:27.726 --> 00:02:28.663
Bueller?

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:35.190
Vocal variation adds excitement
and emphasis to speech.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.920
Then there's language.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.910
Here I mean the choice of words

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:44.400
and the rhetorical
devices the speaker uses

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.470
to appeal to their audience

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:50.020
and make the audience
agree with their points.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.390
So, for example, if you're
at a business convention,

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:58.220
you would expect a speaker
to use pretty formal language

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.770
like, good evening my esteemed colleagues,

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.600
tonight I want to discuss the
importance of market research

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.240
and connecting with our customer base.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.347
You wouldn't expect the speaker to say,

00:03:11.347 --> 00:03:14.467
"(indistinct), I'm a Hanker
and (indistinct) tonight.

00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:16.720
"Let's go mine some gold."

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.870
But if you were at a
gold miners convention,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.300
maybe that's exactly what
you would expect to hear.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:25.560
Language also includes
the rhetorical devices

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.690
that help to structure an argument.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.780
For example, repetition.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.720
Repeating the same word or
phrase can help hammer it home

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.280
or make the speaker's
point feel more urgent.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.090
In Martin Luther King Jr's
"I have a dream" speech,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.227
he starts eight sentences
in a row with the phrase,

00:03:46.227 --> 00:03:47.860
"I have a dream."

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:52.130
So these two points cover
how a speaker talks,

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.740
but there are also physical
aspects to giving a speech.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.570
Gestures and eye contact, visual aids,

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:04.110
and the use of the stage area itself.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.360
The speaker can pace back and forth

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.070
to show they're feeling agitated,

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.270
or throw their shoulders back
to show they're feeling proud.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.610
They can use their hands to
emphasize what they're saying.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.470
Speakers can use props if they want to,

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.250
a chart or image or an object.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:25.890
And just like actors can have
performances that are great,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.510
and performances that are only so, so.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:32.210
Speakers can use all of these
techniques to great effect,

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:36.930
or they can miss the mark by
seeming phony or uncomfortable.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:41.000
So let's take a look at
a speech and its delivery

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.570
to see if we can spot
any of these techniques

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.890
and what messages they convey.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.800
This is a speech from 1988

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.620
by the then governor of Texas Ann Richards

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.150
at the Democratic National Convention.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.010
I'll fast forward to the good parts.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.950
- Those were real people
with real problems

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:01.410
and they had real dreams

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.210
about getting out of the depression.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.960
I can remember summer
nights when we had put down

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.280
what we call the Baptist palette

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.693
and we listened to the grownups talk.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.370
I can still hear the sound of the dominoes

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:17.960
clicking on the marble slab

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.470
my daddy had found for a tabletop.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:21.860
- [Narrator] Did you
see what she did there?

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.450
What's her language telling
us about who she is?

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.460
What did she do with her hands there

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.770
when she talked about the dominoes?

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.810
She's telling us she's not fancy,

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:33.860
she's from an ordinary background

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.530
and she helps us visualize the scene

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.900
by wiggling her fingers like
she's putting down dominoes.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.310
- I got a letter last week

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.830
from a young mother in Lorena, Texas,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.438
and I wanna read part of it to you.

00:05:47.438 --> 00:05:49.260
- [Narrator] Okay. How about that?

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.290
What did you think about
her opening up that letter?

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.750
Is that actually the letter she received

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.440
from the young mother?

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.210
Probably not. I think I
can see the text on it

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.160
and it's really big.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:03.210
So, she's probably
reading from a typed copy.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.080
It's folded in thirds though,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.430
like a letter that came in an envelope.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.970
It gives you the impression
that this is a real letter,

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.280
something she got in the mail,

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.040
and she's telling you
the plain truth from it.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.600
This is a visual aid. Okay.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.020
Let's skip the middle of the letter

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:23.047
and see how Governor Richards
delivers the end of it.

00:06:23.047 --> 00:06:26.733
- We plot along trying to
make it better for ourselves

00:06:26.733 --> 00:06:31.060
and our children and our parents.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.470
We aren't vocal anymore.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:36.173
I think maybe we're too tired.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:43.290
I believe that people like
us are forgotten in America.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.370
- [Narrator] What did you notice about

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:50.990
how Governor Richards delivered
those last two sentences?

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:54.740
I think maybe we're too tired.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.970
She sounds tired.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.390
And she pauses between the words.

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:04.390
I believe that people like
us are forgotten in America.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:08.990
All of those pauses
give weight and emphasis

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:11.030
to the experience of the young mother.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.720
This is vocal variety.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:15.932
Okay. This is the last
one we'll talk about.

00:07:15.932 --> 00:07:18.719
- They've told farmers
that they were selfish,

00:07:18.719 --> 00:07:21.230
that they would drive up food prices

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:23.080
if they asked the government to intervene

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:25.150
on behalf of the family farm

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:27.730
and we watch farms go on the auction block

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:30.620
while we bought food
from foreign countries.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:31.804
Well, that's wrong.

00:07:31.804 --> 00:07:34.554
(crowd applauds)

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:48.630
They told working mothers
it's all their fault

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.560
that families are falling apart

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.510
because they had to go to work
to keep their kids in jeans

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:56.140
and tennis shoes in
college and they're wrong.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.890
(crowd applauds)

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:06.420
They told American labor

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.210
they were trying to ruin free enterprise

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:13.690
by asking for 60 days
notice of plant closings

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:14.913
and that's wrong.

00:08:14.913 --> 00:08:17.370
(crowd applauds)

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.370
- [Narrator] What did you
hear repeated several times

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:21.630
in this section?

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.320
That's wrong.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:25.960
This is a rhetorical device.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:27.320
What does it make you feel

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:30.700
about the points Governor
Richards was making here?

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.220
It kind of feels like they're
piling on each other, right?

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:38.810
She's adding more and
more evidence to her case.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.560
So the next time you watch a speech,

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.970
keep these techniques in mind.

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:46.940
They'll help you see
how a compelling speaker

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.820
can transform the text of the speech

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.593
with vocal and physical effects.

